<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
PrivacyFence autostart Task Scheduler task definition (Phase 3.1, revised).

installer/privacyfence.iss's [Code] section extracts this template at
install time, substitutes __EXEC_PATH__ with the real installed
"{app}\{#AliasExeName}" path, and registers it via
`schtasks /create /tn "PrivacyFence" /xml <file> /f`.

Structure follows Microsoft's own official "any group member" logon
trigger example (learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/taskschd/
logon-trigger-example xml) as closely as possible, deliberately: an
earlier, more elaborate version of this file (every Settings element a
real exported task commonly carries, e.g. IdleSettings and
ExecutionTimeLimit) built and installed without a compile error but
the task still never actually appeared afterward, one plausible
explanation being an element this repo could not verify against a
real schema validator locally. Kept to only the elements this task
actually needs changed from their own documented defaults, closest to
the confirmed official example, to minimize that risk.

Three details are copied from that example verbatim rather than
simplified away, because each one is load bearing and a previous
revision of this file dropped all three at once:

  * `version="1.2"` on the root element. The Settings elements below
    (MultipleInstancesPolicy, RestartOnFailure) are task schema 1.2
    constructs; a definition that does not declare the version is
    validated against the 1.0 schema, where they do not exist.
  * `id` on Principal, and the matching `Context` on Actions. The
    schema links an action set to the security context it runs under
    by that id reference. With a Principal that carries no id and an
    Actions element that names no Context, the GroupId below is not
    actually bound to anything that runs.
  * The trigger's StartBoundary. LogonTrigger inherits it from the
    abstract trigger type, where it is the point from which the
    trigger is live at all.

LogonTrigger with no UserId fires for any interactive logon, not just
one specific account, and Principal uses GroupId (the built-in Users
group) rather than UserId so the task runs as whichever user just
logged on, in their own session: the same "whichever account is at the
keyboard" scope the macOS LaunchAgent and the Linux .deb's XDG
autostart already have.
MultipleInstancesPolicy is Parallel, not the schema default IgnoreNew,
deliberately: this task can legitimately have more than one concurrent
instance at once, one per user session, since GroupId, unlike a single
UserId, can trigger for several different logged on users at the same
time. RestartOnFailure is the piece plain schtasks /create CLI flags
never had a way to express at all; there is no equivalent flag, see
installer/privacyfence.iss's own comment history. It is the direct
analogue of the macOS LaunchAgent's KeepAlive/SuccessfulExit=false and
the Linux .deb plan's own systemd Restart=on-failure. Count is 3, the
same value Task Scheduler's own UI offers by default, rather than an
arbitrarily large number: the restart count is a bounded schema type,
so a large value is a validation risk for no practical gain, and a
daemon that has failed to stay up across three one minute retries is
not going to be rescued by a fourth.

NOTE: this comment intentionally avoids the two-hyphen sequence, which
XML comments may not contain anywhere in their body.
-->
<Task version="1.2" xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/windows/2004/02/mit/task">
  <RegistrationInfo>
    <Description>Starts PrivacyFence when any user signs in, and restarts it if it exits unexpectedly.</Description>
  </RegistrationInfo>
  <Triggers>
    <LogonTrigger>
      <StartBoundary>2020-01-01T00:00:00</StartBoundary>
      <Enabled>true</Enabled>
    </LogonTrigger>
  </Triggers>
  <Principals>
    <Principal id="Author">
      <GroupId>Builtin\Users</GroupId>
      <RunLevel>LeastPrivilege</RunLevel>
    </Principal>
  </Principals>
  <Settings>
    <MultipleInstancesPolicy>Parallel</MultipleInstancesPolicy>
    <Enabled>true</Enabled>
    <AllowStartOnDemand>true</AllowStartOnDemand>
    <AllowHardTerminate>true</AllowHardTerminate>
    <RestartOnFailure>
      <Interval>PT1M</Interval>
      <Count>3</Count>
    </RestartOnFailure>
  </Settings>
  <Actions Context="Author">
    <Exec>
      <Command>__EXEC_PATH__</Command>
    </Exec>
  </Actions>
</Task>
